/* book exam traps: Adding the axial stress and the first-order bending stress and stopping there. The axial load also increases the moment, and that amplified moment often controls the design. | Using PE for pinned ends when the real member has different end restraint. The effective length changes PE, and the amplification 1/(1 − P/PE) changes with it. | Mixing units in the bending stress: M in N m needs c in m and I in m⁴. A value of I in mm⁴ must be multiplied by 10⁻¹². | Applying 1/(1 − P/PE) to every transverse load or end moment. It is exact only for a sine-shaped first-order moment; it is close for a central load and approximate for other cases. Equal end moments need the secant form. | Using the formula above P = PE. The member has then buckled; the amplification is meaningless and a second-order or buckling analysis is required. */
