/* book exam traps: Dropping ε3. The plane-stress formula ε1 = (σ1 − νσ2)/E is wrong when a real third stress exists, as in a shrink-fitted bush or thick forging. | Comparing a signed stress with σY. σ3 = −150 MPa passes a 250 MPa tensile check but fails a 120 MPa compressive allowable. | Taking σ1 as the stress of largest magnitude. σ1 is algebraically largest; σ3 is most compressive, and its strain may be largest in magnitude. | Reading ε1E as a stress in the part. It is a number on the yield-strength scale, not a stress for Mohr's circle. | Unit slip: E in GPa with stresses in MPa makes every strain 1000 times too large and the factor of safety 1000 times too small. Write E as 70 000 MPa, not 70. */
